    {
      "publicId": "chal_quiz_devf_311",
      "name": "ci-needs-if-cache-quiz",
      "type": "quiz",
      "title": "needs, if: and caches in a GitHub Actions workflow",
      "description": "Read two workflow snippets the way the runner does: which jobs run after a failure, and what a cache key really restores and saves.",
      "difficulty": "medium",
      "points": 10,
      "questions": [
        {
          "type": "single_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "A push runs this workflow. The step in the test job exits with code 1. Which jobs run?\n\njobs:\n  test:\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n      - run: pytest\n  build:\n    needs: test\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: docker build -t api .\n  deploy:\n    needs: build\n    if: always()\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    steps:\n      - run: ./deploy.sh",
          "explanation": "build has no if:, so it gets the default if: success(): it only runs when every job in its needs succeeded. test failed, so build is skipped. deploy has if: always(), which replaces that default check: always() is true whatever happened to the jobs it needs (failed, skipped or even cancelled), so deploy runs although nothing was built or tested - the same mistake as in the incident. \"Only test runs\" would be right without the if: always() line. \"build and deploy both run\" ignores that needs + the default success() skip build. A failing job does not cancel the whole run by itself: jobs that do not depend on it keep running (fail-fast only cancels the other jobs of the same matrix). And deploy is not skipped \"because build was skipped\": with always() the status of build no longer matters.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "Only test runs; build and deploy are skipped.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "test fails, build is skipped, and deploy runs.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "test fails, then build and deploy both run.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "GitHub cancels the whole run as soon as test fails, so nothing else runs.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "test fails, build is skipped, and deploy is skipped because build did not run.",
              "correct": false
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "points": 5,
          "question": "The test job restores pip's cache like this. Which statements are true? (Select all that apply.)\n\n- uses: actions/cache@v4\n  id: deps\n  with:\n    path: ~/.cache/pip\n    key: deps-${{ runner.os }}-${{ hashFiles('requirements.txt') }}\n    restore-keys: |\n      deps-${{ runner.os }}-\n- run: pip install -r requirements.txt",
          "explanation": "hashFiles() returns a SHA-256 hash of the file contents, so the key only changes when requirements.txt changes - not on every commit. When it changes, the exact key misses; restore-keys then restores the most recent cache whose key starts with deps-Linux-, pip install downloads only what is new, and the post step saves a new cache under the new exact key. Caches are immutable: a key that already exists is never overwritten, which is why the key must contain a hash of what you depend on. A cache is a best-effort speed-up that GitHub may evict (unused for 7 days, or over the repository's size limit), so it is the wrong way to hand the tested image or wheel to the deploy job - that is what artifacts (or a registry with an immutable tag) are for. Because pip install still runs on every job, a restored cache can only make it faster, never leave stale packages behind; the incident happened because the install step was skipped on a cache hit with a key that never changed.",
          "options": [
            {
              "text": "The key stays the same from commit to commit as long as the contents of requirements.txt do not change.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "After requirements.txt changes, the exact key misses, an older cache is restored through restore-keys, and the job saves a new cache under the new key.",
              "correct": true
            },
            {
              "text": "If a cache with the same key already exists, the end of the job overwrites it with the newer files.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "The cache is a good way to pass the built wheel from the build job to the deploy job, because it is shared by every job of the repository.",
              "correct": false
            },
            {
              "text": "Because pip install runs on every job, a restored older cache cannot leave the job with outdated packages.",
              "correct": true
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
